will consider each pair of digits and check if their sum is divisible by 3.\n\n4. **List all pairs and their sums:**\n   - \\(2 + 3 = 5\\) (not divisible by 3)\n   - \\(2 + 5 = 7\\) (not divisible by 3)\n   - \\(2 + 7 = 9\\) (divisible by 3)\n   - \\(2 + 9 = 11\\) (not divisible by 3)\n   - \\(3 + 5 = 8\\) (not divisible by 3)\n   - \\(3 + 7 = 10\\) (not divisible by 3)\n   - \\(3 + 9 = 12\\) (divisible by 3)\n   - \\(5 + 7 = 12\\) (divisible by 3)\n   - \\(5 + 9 = 14\\) (not divisible by 3)\n   - \\(7 + 9 = 16\\) (not divisible by 3)\n\n5. **Count the pairs whose sums are divisible by 3:**\n   - The pairs are \\((2, 7)\\), \\((3, 9)\\), and \\((5, 7)\\).\n   - There are 3 such pairs.\n\n6. **Form the two-digit numbers from these pairs:**\n   - From \\((2, 7)\\): \\(27\\) and \\(72\\)\n   - From \\((3, 9)\\): \\(39\\) and \\(93\\)\n   - From \\((5, 7)\\): \\(57\\) and \\(75\\)\n\n7. **Count the total number of two-digit numbers divisible by 3:**\n   - There are 6 such numbers: \\(27, 72, 39, 93, 57, 75\\).\n\nThus, the number of two-digit numbers Alice can construct that are divisible by 3 is \\(\\boxed{6}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{6}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"number_theory:34"}
